{
    "title": "Environmental Protection Agency Acquisition Regulation (EPAAR); Special Contracting Methods; Options; Contracts; Withdrawal",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 01, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-01/pdf/2025-21713.pdf",
    "summary": "The government just issued several year-end updates that touch music royalties, satellite TV fees, an EPA contracting rule, and a short fishery closure. Together, they adjust payments, respond to public feedback, and aim to protect a stressed fish stock.\n\nSongwriters and music publishers will see a small boost in 2026 for songs released on CDs, vinyl, and permanent downloads. The statutory rate rises to 13.1 cents per song, or 2.52 cents per minute if that\u2019s higher. This helps creators and rights holders by nudging earnings up with inflation. On the other side, record labels and retailers that produce and sell physical formats and downloads will face slightly higher costs per track, which could trickle into pricing or margins.\n\nSatellite TV providers will also see a modest change. The government applied a 2.9 percent cost-of-living increase to the fees for carrying distant TV signals. In 2026, the monthly rate becomes 37 cents per residential subscriber and 76 cents for commercial locations. Copyright owners benefit from the inflation catch-up, while satellite carriers absorb higher costs they may pass through to customers. The increase is small, but it adds to providers\u2019 overall cost pressures.\n\nThe EPA pulled back a recent contracting rule after receiving critical comments. With the direct final rule withdrawn, the agency will likely revisit the proposal through the regular process. This protects stakeholders who raised concerns and preserves the status quo for now. Contractors and procurement teams who were expecting clearer or updated options language may be disappointed by the delay and lingering uncertainty.\n\nFinally, to prevent overfishing, federal waters in the South Atlantic are closed to commercial gag grouper harvest for the rest of December 2025, after the annual catch limit was met. This supports long-term stock health and sustainability. It also means short-term pain for commercial fishers, seafood dealers, and coastal businesses that rely on late-year sales of gag, and consumers may see tighter supply. The next commercial season opens May 1, 2026, with a higher quota set for that year.\n\nOverall, these moves are incremental but meaningful: small inflation updates to royalties, a reset in EPA rulemaking, and a conservation step for a vulnerable fishery. Beneficiaries gain stability and inflation protection, while those paying the fees or losing fishing days face higher costs or missed revenue in the near term.",
